# "Data and setup guide" and "Method notes" for each analysis.

iv_settings <- function(rows) {
    paste0("<table style='border-collapse:collapse;margin:4px 0 8px 0'>",
           "<tr><th style='text-align:left;padding:2px 14px 2px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>Field</th>",
           "<th style='text-align:left;padding:2px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>Set to</th></tr>",
           paste0("<tr><td style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0'>", names(rows), "</td><td style='padding:2px 0'>",
                  unlist(rows), "</td></tr>", collapse = ""), "</table>")
}

iv_guide_raw <- function(key) {
    switch(key,
    iv = paste0(
        "<h3>IV Regression: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>Estimates the effect of one or more endogenous regressors on an outcome, using excluded instruments that move the regressors but affect the outcome only through them.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data 1: cigarette demand</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Cigarette demand (Stock and Watson)</b>", "Outcome" = "lq",
                         "Endogenous regressors" = "lp", "Excluded instruments" = "tdiff, tax_r", "Exogenous controls" = "li",
                         "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>Expected: the price elasticity is -1.277 with robust s.e. 0.250, the robust first-stage F is about 210, and the overidentification test does not reject (Hansen J = 0.361, p = 0.548).</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data 2: returns to schooling</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>College distance and wages</b>", "Outcome" = "wage", "Endogenous regressors" = "education",
                         "Excluded instruments" = "distance, tuition", "Exogenous controls" = "gender, ethnicity (Nominal), unemp",
                         "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>Expected: the IV estimate of the return to a year of education is 0.915 (robust s.e. 0.163) against 0.0055 for OLS, and the Hansen J test rejects strongly (J = 111.6): at least one of the two instruments is not valid for this outcome.</p>",
        "<h4>Data layout</h4><p>One row per observation. Outcome, endogenous regressors and instruments must be numeric. ",
        "Controls may be numeric or nominal (nominal variables become dummies). Absorbed fixed effects and cluster variables may be nominal, ID or numeric.</p>",
        "<ul><li>You need at least as many excluded instruments as endogenous regressors.</li>",
        "<li>Rows with a missing value in any used variable are dropped and counted.</li>",
        "<li>Weights are analytic weights (as in Stata's <i>aweight</i>): rescaling them changes nothing.</li></ul>"),
    ivfirst = paste0(
        "<h3>First Stage and Weak Instruments: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>Checks whether the excluded instruments are strong enough to identify the endogenous regressors.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Simulated two endogenous regressors</b>", "Outcome" = "y", "Endogenous regressors" = "x1, x2",
                         "Excluded instruments" = "z1, z2, z3, z4", "Exogenous controls" = "w", "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>Same variable roles as IV Regression. Rows with missing values are dropped.</p>"),
    ivweak = paste0(
        "<h3>Weak-Instrument-Robust Inference: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>Tests and confidence sets for the coefficient of one endogenous regressor that stay valid when the instruments are weak.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Simulated weak instruments</b>", "Outcome" = "y", "Endogenous regressor" = "x",
                         "Excluded instruments" = "z1, z2, z3", "Exogenous controls" = "w", "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>The first-stage F is about 5. The true coefficient is 0.5; the conventional 2SLS interval is too narrow, the robust sets are wider.</p>"),
    ivcmp = paste0(
        "<h3>Compare IV Estimators: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>Runs several estimators on the same data and shows them side by side.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Simulated weak instruments</b>", "Outcome" = "y", "Endogenous regressors" = "x",
                         "Excluded instruments" = "z1, z2, z3", "Exogenous controls" = "w", "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>With weak instruments 2SLS is pulled towards OLS and LIML and Fuller differ from it; with the true coefficient 0.5 the estimators spread out.</p>"),
    ivlate = paste0(
        "<h3>Binary Instrument and LATE: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>For a binary treatment moved by a binary instrument: the local average treatment effect for compliers, the shares of always-takers, never-takers and compliers, and how they differ in covariates.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Family size and labour supply (Angrist and Evans)</b>", "Outcome" = "weeks",
                         "Binary treatment" = "morekids (more than two children)", "Binary instrument" = "samesex (first two children of the same sex)",
                         "Covariates to compare" = "age, boy1, afam, hispanic, other", "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>Expected: LATE -6.31 weeks (robust s.e. 1.27); first stage 0.0675, so 6.8% compliers, 34.6% always-takers and 58.6% never-takers; OLS would give -5.39.</p>",
        "<p>Treatment and instrument must be coded 0/1. A variable with two named levels is recoded with the second level as 1.</p>"),
    ivplaus = paste0(
        "<h3>Plausibly Exogenous Instruments: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>Shows how far the exclusion restriction can fail before the conclusion changes.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Cigarette demand (Stock and Watson)</b>", "Outcome" = "lq", "Endogenous regressors" = "lp",
                         "Excluded instruments" = "tdiff, tax_r", "Exogenous controls" = "li", "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)", "delta" = "0.05")),
        "<p>Expected: the 2SLS estimate is -1.277; with direct effects up to 0.05 per instrument the union interval is [-7.00, 4.76]; the tipping delta is 0.0074.</p>"),
    ivover = paste0(
        "<h3>Overidentification and Endogeneity: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>Tests whether the instruments are valid and whether the regressors need instrumenting at all.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Simulated two endogenous regressors</b>", "Outcome" = "y", "Endogenous regressors" = "x1, x2",
                         "Excluded instruments" = "z1, z2, z4", "Exogenous controls" = "w",
                         "Instruments to test" = "z3", "Standard errors" = "Robust (HC1)")),
        "<p>A variable can be in one box only: put the instruments you trust under Excluded instruments and the ones you want to test under Instruments to test; ",
        "both groups are used as instruments. The same holds for Regressors to test.</p>",
        "<p>The simulated instrument z3 is correlated with the error, so the overidentification test should reject and the C test for z3 should flag it.</p>"),
    ivmte = paste0(
        "<h3>Marginal Treatment Effects: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>When people choose the treatment, the effect differs between people and the ones at the margin of choosing differ from the rest. The marginal treatment effect (MTE; Heckman and Vytlacil, 2005) is the average effect for people who are just indifferent between treatment and no treatment, as a function of their unobserved <i>resistance</i> u to treatment (u near 0: those who take the treatment even if it is unattractive to most). It needs a binary treatment and instruments that move the propensity to take it over a wide range. The treatment effect parameters (ATE, ATT, ATUT) are averages of the MTE.</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Simulated schooling choice (marginal treatment effects)</b>", "Outcome" = "logwage", "Binary treatment (0/1)" = "college", "Instruments" = "distance, tuition", "Covariates" = "score, female",
                         "Degree of the polynomial" = "1", "First stage" = "Probit", "Bootstrap samples" = "300")),
        "<p>6,000 people, 54% go to college; returns fall with the unobserved resistance to going (the true marginal effect is 0.45 + 0.10 score - 0.30 times the normal quantile of u, so the true ATE is 0.453, the ATT about 0.65 and the ATUT about 0.22). ",
        "Expected: the propensity score has mean 0.540 (treated 0.660, untreated 0.398) and ranges from 0.000 to 0.995. ATE 0.455 (s.e. 0.041, interval 0.376 to 0.535), ATT 0.610 (0.068), ATUT 0.274 (0.091), 2SLS 0.498 (0.044). ",
        "The marginal effect falls from 0.762 at u = 0.05 to 0.626 at 0.25, 0.455 at 0.50, 0.285 at 0.75 and 0.149 at 0.95. Test of essential heterogeneity: chi-square 4.14, p 0.042; of observed heterogeneity: p 0.001. ",
        "With the degree 2 polynomial: ATE 0.440 (s.e. 0.070), ATT 0.601, ATUT 0.251 and the test of essential heterogeneity has p 0.12: higher degrees are less precise and need more data.</p>",
        "<h4>Data layout</h4><p>One row per person. The treatment is 0/1 (or two-level text, the second level being treated). Instruments must not affect the outcome except through the treatment and must be unrelated to the unobserved resistance, given the covariates. Rows with missing values are dropped.</p>"),
    shiftshare = paste0(
        "<h3>Shift-Share (Bartik) IV: data and setup</h3>",
        "<p>IV regression where the instrument is built from sector-level shocks and each region's exposure to them, with standard errors that allow for the correlation this creates between regions that have similar exposure (Adao, Kolesar and Morales, 2019).</p>",
        "<h4>Sample data: simulated regions and sectors</h4>",
        iv_settings(list("Dataset" = "<b>Simulated shift-share design</b>", "Outcome" = "y", "Endogenous regressor" = "d", "Region" = "region",
                         "Sector (shock)" = "sector", "Exposure share" = "share", "Shock" = "shock", "Controls" = "x1", "Weights" = "pop",
                         "Region cluster" = "state", "Sector cluster" = "sector_group")),
        "<p>500 regions, 40 sectors, 8 sectors per region (4,000 rows); the true coefficient is 0.5. Expected: IV coefficient 0.513; standard errors 0.124 (homoskedastic), 0.145 (robust), 0.104 (state clusters), ",
        "0.188 (AKM, 8 sector groups) and AKM0 confidence set 0.046 to 1.583; first stage coefficient 0.938 with AKM s.e. 0.065 (F 207); the effective number of sectors is 39.2. ",
        "OLS is -0.20 because the unobserved regional factor drives both regressor and outcome. The standard errors that ignore the shift-share structure are too small.</p>",
        "<h4>Data layout</h4><p><b>Long form: one row per region and sector</b> that has a positive exposure share. ",
        "Illustrative example:</p>",
        "<table style='border-collapse:collapse;margin:4px 0 8px 0'><tr><th style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>region</th><th style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>sector</th><th style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>share</th><th style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>shock</th><th style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>y</th><th style='padding:2px 14px 2px 0;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #999'>d</th></tr>",
        "<tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>0.40</td><td>1.2</td><td>0.8</td><td>2.1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>0.60</td><td>-0.5</td><td>0.8</td><td>2.1</td></tr>",
        "<tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.2</td><td>-0.3</td><td>0.4</td></tr></table>",
        "<ul><li>Region-level variables (outcome, regressor, controls, weights, region cluster) repeat on every row of a region; the shock and the sector cluster repeat on every row of a sector. The module checks this.</li>",
        "<li>The instrument of region l is the sum over its sectors of share times shock. Sectors with no exposure need no row.</li>",
        "<li>If the shares do not add up to the same number in every region, tick <i>Control for the sum of the shares</i>.</li>",
        "<li>Rows with missing values are dropped; a region with a missing region-level value is dropped whole.</li></ul>"),

    "")
}

iv_notes_html <- function(key) {
    switch(key,
    iv = paste0(
        "<h3>IV Regression: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>Instrumental-variable regression estimates the coefficients of endogenous regressors using excluded instruments. ",
        "The table of second-stage estimates shows the endogenous regressors and, if ticked, the controls. With one binary instrument for a binary treatment, ",
        "the estimate is a local average treatment effect for compliers.</p>",
        "<h4>Options</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>Estimator</b>: 2SLS; LIML (less biased with many or weak instruments); Fuller with parameter alpha (alpha = 1 is the usual choice); a general k-class (k = 1 is 2SLS, k = 0 is OLS); two-step efficient GMM (needs robust or cluster-robust errors).</li>",
        "<li><b>Standard errors</b>: classical; robust (HC1); cluster-robust on the first cluster variable; two-way cluster-robust using both. ",
        "With few clusters (about 30 or fewer) cluster-robust errors can be unreliable.</li>",
        "<li><b>Small-sample corrections</b>: on, the variance is scaled as in Stata's <i>small</i> option and tests use t and F distributions; off, large-sample normal and chi-square.</li>",
        "<li><b>Absorbed fixed effects</b>: one or two sets, removed by within transformation; their degrees of freedom are counted except when nested in the first cluster variable.</li>",
        "<li><b>Weights</b>: analytic weights.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>How to read the results</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>OLS compared with IV</b>: a large difference suggests the regressor is endogenous (or the instrument is weak or invalid).</li>",
        "<li><b>First stage</b>: F tests the excluded instruments jointly. Below about 10 the instruments are weak and 2SLS can be badly biased; use the weak-instrument-robust analyses (planned) or LIML/Fuller.</li>",
        "<li><b>Overidentification test</b>: Sargan with classical errors, Hansen J with robust or cluster errors. Rejection says that at least one instrument is invalid or the effects differ across instruments; it cannot say which. ",
        "It is shown only when there are more instruments than endogenous regressors and, for cluster errors, more clusters than instruments.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>Instrument validity (independence and the exclusion restriction) cannot be tested, only probed. ",
        "Not included yet: weak-instrument-robust confidence sets, Kleibergen-Paap and effective F statistics, endogeneity tests and sensitivity to exclusion-restriction violations.</p>"),
    ivfirst = paste0(
        "<h3>First Stage and Weak Instruments: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>Instruments are weak when they explain little of the endogenous regressors once the controls are removed. ",
        "Weak instruments make 2SLS biased towards OLS and its standard errors and tests unreliable.</p>",
        "<h4>Statistics</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>F (one regressor at a time)</b>: Wald test that the excluded instruments are jointly zero in the first stage, using the chosen standard errors. ",
        "<b>Partial R2</b> is the share of the regressor's variation explained by the excluded instruments after the controls.</li>",
        "<li><b>Conditional F (Sanderson-Windmeijer)</b>: with several endogenous regressors a large joint statistic can hide a regressor that is not separately identified; ",
        "this F removes the other regressors first.</li>",
        "<li><b>Cragg-Donald Wald F</b>: the smallest eigenvalue of the first-stage F matrix under homoskedastic errors; equals the first-stage F with one regressor.</li>",
        "<li><b>Kleibergen-Paap rk Wald F</b>: the analogue allowing heteroskedastic or cluster errors (shown with robust or cluster standard errors). ",
        "The chi-square version tests underidentification.</li>",
        "<li><b>Effective F (Montiel Olea and Pflueger)</b>: a heteroskedasticity-robust F for one endogenous regressor whose critical values do not depend on the error structure. ",
        "The simplified critical values (valid for 2SLS and LIML, 5% level, bias thresholds 5, 10, 20 and 30% of the benchmark) are shown; the sharper 2SLS- and LIML-specific values are not computed.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Reduced form</h4><p>The outcome regressed on the excluded instruments and controls. If the reduced form is weak or zero, an IV estimate is not informative.</p>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>Stock-Yogo critical values and weak-instrument-robust confidence sets (Anderson-Rubin, CLR) are not part of this analysis.</p>"),
    ivweak = paste0(
        "<h3>Weak-Instrument-Robust Inference: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>With weak instruments the usual 2SLS t test and interval are unreliable. The tests here keep their size whatever the instrument strength. ",
        "The confidence set is every coefficient value that the test does not reject.</p>",
        "<h4>Tests</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>Anderson-Rubin (AR)</b>: tests that the instruments are uncorrelated with y - b x. Valid under weak identification and robust to which instruments are weak, but loses power when there are many instruments.</li>",
        "<li><b>Kleibergen (K)</b>: a score test with one degree of freedom; more powerful than AR with many instruments but can lose power at some coefficient values, producing odd sets.</li>",
        "<li><b>Conditional likelihood ratio (CLR)</b>: combines AR and K; its p-value is computed conditional on the rank statistic. It is the usual recommendation with heteroskedastic or clustered errors as well, using the same conditional p-value function as an approximation.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Options</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>Hypothesised coefficient</b>: the value tested in the first table.</li>",
        "<li><b>Standard errors</b>: classical, robust or cluster-robust moment covariances (the small-sample option affects only the 2SLS Wald interval).</li>",
        "<li><b>Grid half-width</b>: the sets are found by evaluating the tests on a grid around the 2SLS estimate; widen the grid if a bound is shown as Inf.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>One endogenous regressor only. The sets can be wide, disjoint or unbounded; that is information about weak identification, not an error. tF adjustments are not included.</p>"),
    ivcmp = paste0(
        "<h3>Compare IV Estimators: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>Estimates the same equation with OLS, 2SLS, LIML, Fuller (modified LIML) and optionally two-step GMM, with the chosen standard errors, and reports the endogenous coefficients and intervals in a table and a forest plot.</p>",
        "<h4>How to read it</h4><ul><li>OLS versus IV: a large gap suggests endogeneity (or invalid or weak instruments).</li>",
        "<li>2SLS versus LIML and Fuller: LIML and Fuller are less biased with many or weak instruments; a large gap warns that the instruments are weak.</li>",
        "<li>The last column gives the overidentification test of each IV estimator where it is defined (2SLS and two-step GMM).</li></ul>"),
    ivlate = paste0(
        "<h3>Binary Instrument and LATE: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>With a binary treatment D and a binary instrument Z, the Wald ratio (reduced form / first stage), which equals the 2SLS estimate, is the average effect for compliers if the instrument is as good as randomly assigned, affects the outcome only through the treatment, and never pushes anyone out of treatment (monotonicity).</p>",
        "<h4>Groups</h4><ul><li><b>Always-takers</b> are treated whatever the instrument; their share is the treatment rate when the instrument is off.</li>",
        "<li><b>Never-takers</b> are untreated whatever the instrument; their share is the untreated rate when the instrument is on.</li>",
        "<li><b>Compliers</b> are treated only when the instrument is on; their share is the first-stage effect.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Covariate means</h4><p>Always- and never-taker means are observed directly; complier means follow from the overall mean (Abadie 2003). They show whom the LATE speaks for. Controls change the reduced form, first stage and LATE, but not the shares and means.</p>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>Monotonicity and the exclusion restriction cannot be tested here. A negative estimated share points to a violation.</p>"),
    ivplaus = paste0(
        "<h3>Plausibly Exogenous Instruments: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>The exclusion restriction says the instruments affect the outcome only through the endogenous regressors. If each instrument instead has a direct effect g, the 2SLS coefficient shifts by a known amount per unit of g. ",
        "Following Conley, Hansen and Rossi (2012) this shows how conclusions change when g is allowed to be non-zero.</p>",
        "<h4>Results</h4><ul><li><b>Tipping delta</b>: the smallest bound on the direct effects (every instrument between -delta and delta) for which the union of confidence intervals contains zero. Judge it against the size of a direct effect you think plausible.</li>",
        "<li><b>Union of confidence intervals</b>: the union over all direct effects in the box of the confidence intervals, computed at the corners of the box with the chosen standard errors (as the Stata command plausexog does); it widens roughly linearly with delta.</li>",
        "<li><b>Local-to-zero approximation</b>: direct effects are treated as normal with a given mean and standard deviation; the estimate is shifted by the implied bias and the variance is enlarged. It gives a narrower interval than the union when the prior is informative.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>Normal critical values are used. All excluded instruments share the same bound; one endogenous regressor is the usual case but several are reported separately.</p>"),
    ivover = paste0(
        "<h3>Overidentification and Endogeneity: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>Sargan / Hansen J</b>: with more instruments than endogenous regressors, tests that all instruments are valid. Sargan under classical errors, Hansen J (robust to heteroskedasticity or clusters) otherwise.</li>",
        "<li><b>C test (difference in Hansen)</b>: tests the instruments listed under Instruments to test (they are used as instruments as well) while assuming the others are valid. ",
        "It needs enough remaining instruments to identify the model.</li>",
        "<li><b>Endogeneity test</b>: compares the model that treats the chosen regressors as endogenous with one that treats them as exogenous. ",
        "Rejection says OLS and IV differ systematically. It is the heteroskedasticity-robust version of the Durbin-Wu-Hausman test.</li>",
        "<li><b>Drop each instrument in turn</b>: a C test per instrument, useful to spot a single suspect instrument.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>How to read the results</h4><p>All tests are chi-square tests built as differences of Hansen J statistics and so are never negative. ",
        "They need the remaining instruments to be valid; a rejection says something is wrong, not which instrument. ",
        "A test that does not reject is weak evidence of validity, because these tests have low power when the instruments are weak.</p>"),
    ivmte = paste0(
        "<h3>Marginal Treatment Effects: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>The model</h4><p>The treatment is taken when the propensity score P(Z, X) exceeds an unobserved resistance U uniformly distributed on (0, 1). The marginal treatment effect MTE(x, u) is the average effect for people with covariates x and resistance u. Under the instrument assumptions it is the derivative of the mean outcome with respect to the propensity score (local instrumental variables). ",
        "Following the Stata command mtefe, the mean outcome is modelled as E[Y | X, P = p] = X b0 + p X (b1 - b0) + K(p), K(p) a polynomial in p from the power 2 to the power L + 1, so that MTE(x, u) = x (b1 - b0) + k(u) is a polynomial of degree L in u that is the same for all x (separability).</p>",
        "<h4>Parameters</h4><ul><li><b>ATE</b>: the average of the MTE over u from 0.01 to 0.99 at the mean of the covariates. <b>ATT</b> and <b>ATUT</b>: averages with weights Pr(P &gt; u) / E[P] and Pr(P &le; u) / (1 - E[P]) at the mean of the covariates among the treated and the untreated (weights p and 1 - p).</li>",
        "<li><b>2SLS</b> is shown for comparison: it is a weighted average of the marginal effects, with weights that depend on the instruments, and generally differs from the ATE.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Inference</h4><p>A bootstrap of units (or of clusters) repeats the first stage and the second stage; standard errors and normal intervals come from it. The tests are Wald tests with the bootstrap covariance matrix: essential heterogeneity (the coefficients of the polynomial K(p)) and observed heterogeneity (the covariates in b1 - b0). A flat MTE means that all parameters coincide with the 2SLS estimate.</p>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>The MTE is identified only over the range of the propensity score; outside the range the curve extrapolates the polynomial (dashed in the plot), and the ATE, ATT and ATUT rely on that extrapolation when the instruments are not strong enough to push the scores close to 0 and 1. Higher degrees are more flexible and less precise. Use strong, valid instruments with a continuous component; the method does not test their validity. Binary treatment only; no sampling weights. The point estimates are those of mtefe for the polynomial model (probit first stage by default); bootstrap standard errors differ from its robust ones, which ignore the first stage.</p>"),
    shiftshare = paste0(
        "<h3>Shift-Share (Bartik) IV: method notes</h3>",
        "<h4>What it does</h4><p>A shift-share instrument combines sector-level shocks g(k) with the exposure shares s(l,k) of each region l: z(l) = sum over k of s(l,k) g(k). ",
        "The coefficient is estimated by two-stage least squares with z as the instrument for the endogenous regressor, with the controls (and the weights) in both stages. ",
        "Identification comes either from the exogeneity of the shares (Goldsmith-Pinkham, Sorkin and Swift, 2020) or from the quasi-random assignment of the shocks (Borusyak, Hull and Jaravel, 2022).</p>",
        "<h4>Standard errors</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>Homoskedastic, robust, region cluster</b>: treat regions (or clusters of regions) as independent. Regions that load on the same sectors share the same shocks, so their errors are correlated and these standard errors are typically too small.</li>",
        "<li><b>AKM</b> (Adao, Kolesar and Morales, 2019): the variance is built from sums over sectors of the share-weighted residuals, so it allows the errors of regions with similar exposure to be correlated. Use it for inference. If you give a <b>sector cluster</b> (for example a broader industry group), the sums are taken over the groups, which also allows the shock errors of related sectors to be correlated.</li>",
        "<li><b>AKM0</b>: the confidence set that imposes the null value of the coefficient in the variance; it has better coverage when the instrument is weak and can be unbounded.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Options</h4><ul>",
        "<li><b>Control for the sum of the shares</b>: when shares do not add up to one (or to the same number) the instrument also moves with the total exposure; Borusyak et al. recommend controlling for it.</li>",
        "<li><b>Value of the coefficient under the null</b>: the p-values test this value (default 0); AKM0 imposes it.</li>",
        "<li><b>First stage and reduced form</b>: the regressions of the endogenous regressor and of the outcome on the instrument, with robust and AKM standard errors; F (AKM) is the squared t statistic of the first stage with the AKM s.e.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>How to read the results</h4><ul>",
        "<li>Compare the AKM s.e. with the robust s.e.: a much larger AKM s.e. means the shift-share structure matters.</li>",
        "<li>The <b>effective number of sectors</b> is the inverse Herfindahl index of the sector exposure weights. Few effective sectors (say below 20) means the asymptotic approximations may be poor.</li></ul>",
        "<h4>Limits</h4><p>One endogenous regressor and one shift-share instrument. Sector-level shocks must be exogenous (or the shares must be); the module does not test this. Results equal the R package ShiftShareSE and the Stata command ivreg_ss.</p>"),

    "")
}

# Adds where to open the example data after the introduction.
iv_guide_html <- function(key) {
    x <- iv_guide_raw(key)
    sub("</p>", paste0("</p><p><i>Open the sample data from File &gt; Open &gt; Data Library &gt; Instrumental Variables Family; ",
                       "the library lists each data set by the bold name below.</i></p>"), x, fixed = TRUE)
}
